      "judgment": {
        "status": "confirmed",
        "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. No earlier submitted answers or grades were supplied. q-132255: the chain rule gives g_z=-2yz\\sqrt{x}/\\sqrt{1-y^2z^4}, which evaluates to -2. q-35715: differentiating arcsin(xy) with respect to y contributes x/\\sqrt{1-x^2y^2}, then multiply by z. q-109084: variance scales by the square of the coefficient, so 3·2=6; translation has no effect. q-129390: the squared coefficient is 1/2, giving 5. q-109226: E[X]=0.07+0.16+0.27+0.08=0.58, so E[0.5X]=0.29. q-109234: linearity gives -6(-3/2)-1=8. q-36623: f_y=-x/(x^2+y^2) wherever the original function is defined, requiring y≠0; simplifying the formula does not extend the original derivative to y=0. q-132639: the logarithm requires z>0 and the denominator requires x+y≠0; f_z=1/[z(x+y)] exists throughout that domain. q-132655: the function and both partial derivatives exist exactly when y≠0. q-65252: the logarithm requires xy>0, which also ensures both supplied partial derivatives exist. q-132732: I and II have zero denominators in the original function; III lies in its smooth domain, so III only. q-72011: I and II have x-y>0 and finite partial derivatives. At (1,1), the x difference quotient behaves as 1/\\sqrt{h} for h approaching zero from above, so no finite partial derivative exists; hence I and II only. q-80400: product and chain rules give 3y^2 sin(1+3xy)+3xy^3 cos(1+3xy). q-65065: the quotient rule gives [6y(x^3+y^3)-9y^4]/(x^3+y^3)^2=3y(2x^3-y^3)/(x^3+y^3)^2. q-80473: g_xx=-4/(2x-y)^2 and g_yy=-1/(2x-y)^2, yielding -5/9. q-80468: each mixed partial equals 2xy sin(x^2-y^2), so their sum is 4xy sin(x^2-y^2).",
        "confidence": 1,
