        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists",
        "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)}{x+y},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists.",
        "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(x+y) & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
