      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-80473",
        "knowledge_point_id": "503a0bfe-7c69-5ad0-ad1d-aa8692dae673",
        "knowledge_point": "Computing Expressions Involving Second-Order Partial Derivatives",
        "problem": "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)+{g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1)$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2}{2x-y}$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2x-y}.$",
        "worked_solution": "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{2}{2x-y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(2x-y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{1}{2x-y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(1,-1) & =-\\frac{4}{(2(1)-(-1))^{2}}=-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {g}_{yy}(1,-1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2(1)-(-1))^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xx}(1,-1)+{g}_{yy}(1,-1)=-\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}=-\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$",
